    }

    .category-header h2 {
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .category-header p {
      color: #555;
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }</style><div class="cfp-section"><h2>JCDL 2026 Workshops and Tutorials</h2><p>
JCDL 2026 workshops and tutorials bring together researchers, practitioners,
educators, and cultural heritage professionals to explore emerging challenges
and practical innovations in digital libraries, artificial intelligence,
web archives, scholarly metadata, and knowledge organization.</p><div class="highlight-box">
📅 <strong>Conference Dates:</strong> October 13–16, 2026<br>
📍 <strong>Conference Format:</strong> Hybrid, with individual workshop and tutorial formats specified below</div></div><hr class="section-divider"><div class="category-header"><h2>Workshops</h2><p>
Workshops provide focused forums for research presentations, discussion,
community exchange, and collaboration on emerging topics in digital libraries.</p></div><div class="cfp-section"><h3>The 3rd International Workshop on Artificial Intelligence and the Science of Science (AI4SciSci)</h3><p>
<span class="tag">Workshop</span>
<span class="tag">Hybrid</span>
<span class="tag">Full Day</span>
<span class="tag">October 13</span></p><h4>Abstract</h4><div class="abstract-box"><p>
Over the past decade, the field of artificial intelligence (AI) has witnessed
significant advancements across domains such as natural language processing
and computer vision. These advances have accelerated discoveries, innovations,
and breakthroughs across interdisciplinary fields, including the Science of
Science, which relies on large-scale data to uncover reproducible patterns
governing the evolution of research topics, collaboration networks, knowledge
diffusion, and systemic factors that drive scientific progress.</p><p>
Science of Science is increasingly incorporating AI techniques into the analysis
of large-scale data, yielding insights into longstanding and emerging problems.
Building on the first and second AI4SciSci workshops, held with ICDM 2023 and
JCDL 2025, this workshop will further engage the AI and Science of Science
communities in discussion and research on frontier topics in this area.</p></div><h4>Organizers</h4><ul class="organizer-list"><li>
<strong><a href="https://www.cs.odu.edu/~jwu/" target="_blank">Jian Wu</a></strong>,
Old Dominion University, United States</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://www.rajtmajerlab.net/" target="_blank">Sarah Rajtmajer</a></strong>,
Pennsylvania State University, United States</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://yhe15.people.wm.edu/" target="_blank">Yi He</a></strong>,
College of William &amp; Mary, United States</li></ul><table class="info-table"><tr><td>Workshop Website</td><td>
<a href="https://ai4scisci.github.io/2026/" target="_blank">
ai4scisci.github.io/2026
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Paper Submission</td><td>
<a href="https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=ai4scisci2026" target="_blank">
EasyChair Submission Portal
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred Date</td><td>October 13, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Full Day</td></tr></table></div><hr class="divider"><div class="cfp-section"><h3>Web Archiving and Digital Libraries (WADL) 2026 Workshop</h3><p>
<span class="tag">Workshop</span>
<span class="tag">Hybrid</span>
<span class="tag">Full Day</span>
<span class="tag">October 16</span></p><h4>Abstract</h4><div class="abstract-box"><p>
The 2026 edition of the Workshop on Web Archiving and Digital Libraries
(WADL) will explore the integration of web archiving and digital libraries.
The workshop addresses the full life cycle of digital resources, including
creation and authoring, uploading and publishing, crawling, indexing,
exploration, and archiving.</p><p>
It will also examine processes and tools for preserving non-traditional
resources, including social media, scholarly and government datasets,
3D objects, and digital online art.</p></div><h4>Organizers</h4><ul class="organizer-list"><li>
<strong><a href="https://matkelly.com" target="_blank">Mat Kelly</a></strong>,
Assistant Professor, Drexel University, United States</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://reyesayala.github.io" target="_blank">Brenda Reyes Ayala</a></strong>,
Associate Professor, University of Alberta, Canada</li></ul><table class="info-table"><tr><td>Workshop Website</td><td>
<a href="https://wadlworkshop.github.io/2026/" target="_blank">
wadlworkshop.github.io/2026
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Paper Submission</td><td><a href="https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=wadl2026" target="_blank">
EasyChair Submission Portal
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred Date</td><td>October 16, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Full Day</td></tr></table></div><hr class="divider"><div class="cfp-section"><h3>Future Libraries in the Age of AI: Service Transformation and Governance Challenges (AI4Lib)</h3><p>
<span class="tag">Workshop</span>
<span class="tag">Hybrid</span>
<span class="tag">Half Day</span>
<span class="tag">October 13</span></p><h4>Abstract</h4><div class="abstract-box"><p>
Artificial intelligence is reshaping libraries from traditional resource
repositories into intelligent, adaptive, and user-centered knowledge service
hubs. Across public, academic, and special libraries, AI is increasingly
embedded in information discovery, reference services, knowledge organization,
user interaction, decision support, and operational workflows.</p><p>
These developments create new opportunities for digital library innovation,
while raising questions about trust, accountability, data privacy, algorithmic
fairness, professional ethics, and inclusive access. This workshop examines
future libraries through the dual lenses of service transformation and
governance challenges, bringing together researchers, practitioners, AI
researchers, and policy-oriented stakeholders to develop sustainable,
equitable, accountable, and human-centered approaches.</p></div><h4>Organizers</h4><ul class="organizer-list"><li>
<strong><a href="https://sim.whu.edu.cn/info/1571/12956.htm" target="_blank">Dan Wu</a></strong>,
Wuhan University, China</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://sim.whu.edu.cn/info/1560/12945.htm" target="_blank">Shaobo Liang</a></strong>,
Wuhan University, China</li></ul><table class="info-table"><tr><td>Workshop Website</td><td>
<a href="https://hci.whu.edu.cn/info/1005/4311.htm" target="_blank">
Workshop Information Page
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Paper Submission</td><td>
<a href="mailto:liangshaobo@whu.edu.cn">
liangshaobo@whu.edu.cn
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred Date</td><td>October 13, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Half Day</td></tr></table></div><hr class="divider"><div class="cfp-section"><h3>Smarter Extraction of Scholarly Metadata using Knowledge Graphs, Language Models, and Agents</h3><p>
<span class="tag">Workshop</span>
<span class="tag">Online Only</span>
<span class="tag">Full Day</span>
<span class="tag">October 16</span></p><h4>Abstract</h4><div class="abstract-box"><p>
Scholarly metadata is essential to the scholarly ecosystem, supporting
discovery, interoperability, and provenance. However, the substantial
growth and diversification of research artifacts—including publications,
datasets, source code, and machine-learning models—makes the collection
of heterogeneous metadata features across repositories increasingly complex.</p><p>
Large language models and agents are being widely adopted for tasks ranging
from information extraction to knowledge-graph construction. Important
questions remain about their accuracy, robustness, bias, and societal impact
in research metadata, scholarly information extraction, and open science.
This workshop explores these questions with a focus on metadata features from
research artifacts, large language models, and knowledge graphs.</p></div><h4>Organizers</h4><ul class="organizer-list"><li>
<strong>Muhammad Asif Suryani</strong>,
Leibniz-Institut für Sozialwissenschaften (GESIS), Köln, Germany</li><li>
<strong>Brigitte Mathiak</strong>,
Leibniz-Institut für Sozialwissenschaften (GESIS), Köln, Germany</li><li>
<strong>Florian Reitz</strong>,
Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik, Wadern, Germany</li><li>
<strong>Florian Jäckel</strong>,
Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik, Wadern, Germany</li><li>
<strong>Florian Hauss</strong>,
Data Science and Big Data Analytics, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany</li><li>
<strong>Ansgar Scherp</strong>,
Data Science and Big Data Analytics, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany</li></ul><table class="info-table"><tr><td>Workshop Website</td><td>
<a href="https://sesame-workshop.github.io/SESAME/" target="_blank">
SESAME Workshop Website
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Paper Submission</td><td>
<a href="https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=sesame26" target="_blank">
EasyChair Submission Portal
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Online Only</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred Date</td><td>October 16, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Full Day</td></tr></table></div><hr class="section-divider"><div class="category-header"><h2>Tutorials</h2><p>
Tutorials provide expert-led, structured instruction and practical,
hands-on learning opportunities for participants.</p></div><div class="cfp-section"><h3>Tiered Affordances in Web Archive Formats (TAWAF): Efficient Analytics from Indexes to Raw Captures at Scale</h3><p>
<span class="tag">Tutorial</span>
<span class="tag">Hybrid</span>
<span class="tag">Half Day</span>
<span class="tag">October 13</span></p><h4>Abstract</h4><div class="abstract-box"><p>
Web archive collections are increasingly large and heterogeneous, making it
challenging for practitioners to choose efficient representations and workflows
for common analytical tasks. This half-day hybrid tutorial provides a hands-on
introduction to web-archiving formats, including WARC, CDX/CDXJ, Parquet, and
derived formats such as WAT and WET, demonstrating how each format supports
different kinds of analysis.</p><p>
Using the End of Term Archive Datasets, comprising more than 2.5 PB, as a
real-world testbed, the tutorial presents a three-tier model of analytical
queries. Participants will access data on AWS S3, convert and query CDX(J)
data in Parquet, use SQL through DuckDB and Python workflows, and perform
link-structure and basic NLP tasks with WAT/WET. The tutorial emphasizes
practical trade-offs among data formats, costs, and computational requirements.</p></div><h4>Organizers</h4><ul class="organizer-list"><li>
<strong><a href="https://x.com/ibnesayeed" target="_blank">Sawood Alam</a></strong>,
Internet Archive, United States</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://x.com/vphill" target="_blank">Mark E. Phillips</a></strong>,
University of North Texas, United States</li></ul><table class="info-table"><tr><td>Tutorial Website</td><td>
<a href="https://github.com/end-of-term/tawaf-tutorial" target="_blank">
GitHub Repository
</a></td></tr><tr><td>Paper Submission</td><td><span class="unavailable">Not applicable</span></td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred Date</td><td>October 13, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Half Day</td></tr></table></div><hr class="divider"><div class="cfp-section"><h3>AI for Organizing Digital Collections: Practical Approaches with LLMs and Vision-Language Models (LLM4Cat)</h3><p>
<span class="tag">Tutorial</span>
<span class="tag">Hybrid</span>
<span class="tag">Half Day</span>
<span class="tag">October 16</span></p><h4>Abstract</h4><div class="abstract-box"><p>
AI for Organizing Digital Collections is a half-day tutorial introducing
practical approaches for applying AI to organize textual, visual, and
multimodal digital collections. It examines how large language models can
support classification, subject analysis, and vocabulary mapping, and how
vision-language models can support archival image description and visual
subject-term assignment.</p><p>
Through accessible explanations, demonstrations, and hands-on exercises,
participants will learn how to train LLMs for digital collection organization,
evaluate model outputs, and design AI-assisted workflows. The tutorial is
intended for digital library researchers, metadata professionals, catalogers,
LIS educators, students, and cultural heritage practitioners.</p></div><h4>Organizers</h4><ul class="organizer-list"><li>
<strong><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jinyu-liu-539a5b363/" target="_blank">Jinyu Liu</a></strong>,
University of North Texas, United States</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://library.unt.edu/people/jason-thomale/" target="_blank">Jason Thomale</a></strong>,
University of North Texas Libraries, United States</li><li>
<strong><a href="https://ci.unt.edu/lingzi-hong/index.html" target="_blank">Lingzi Hong</a></strong>,
University of North Texas, United States</li></ul><table class="info-table"><tr><td>Tutorial Website</td><td><span class="unavailable">To be announced</span></td></tr><tr><td>Paper Submission</td><td><span class="unavailable">Not applicable</span></td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Hybrid</td></tr><tr><td>Preferred Date</td><td>October 16, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Half Day</td></tr></table></div><hr class="divider"><div class="cfp-section"><div class="highlight-box">
📬 <strong>Workshop and Tutorial Inquiries:</strong>
Please contact the respective organizers through the workshop websites,
paper-submission systems, or email addresses listed above.</div></div></div></div><footer class="wp-block-template-part"><div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained" style="padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--50);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--50)"><footer style="
